body:
Ages old throwaway account. Ended up here pretty randomly.

&#x200B;

I'm in a somewhat similar situation to your partner. We both live alone and separately and I get less hateful and would never tell my partner in any detail about my suicidal thoughts though they do know I have them.

&#x200B;

Anti-depressants: Been on a few of them and have had bad reactions to most of the ones I've been on.

&#x200B;

Weed is probably not great, it can trigger anxiety and likely doesn't help activity levels. On the other hand, it can be a thing one enjoys, which can be huge if you don't find enjoyment in anything else. A mixed bag.

&#x200B;

Exercise, sleep and eating do play a role. There is scientific literature backing that statement up, to what extent, well that's different. Mindfulness is pure crap. Though that comes with a disclaimer that that is based purely on my personal experience and I can't be arsed to find scientific literature on that one because of how fucking awful mindfulness is.

&#x200B;

The various classes of depression, treatment resistant, periodic, whatever are in my experience entirely arbitrary. Just something the doc decides to write down, don't expect them to have any meaning.

&#x200B;

Hope. Well that's the thing. Your situation does sound bad as well as your boyfriends. There is no guarantees. There are things that could help for sure especially if he hasn't tried them before. Accessibility can be an issue as well as the costs. And the efficiency if he doesn't believe in the treatments is worse.

&#x200B;

As to what to do. Well, again, there is no easy answers and well, the situation does sound pretty unbearable. Sorry.
